  "solution": {
    "text": "A. <math><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> has 3 unpaired electrons<br /><br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> has 4 unpaired electrons<br /><br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> has 5 unpaired electrons<br /><br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> has 4 unpaired electrons<br /><br />Hence the order of paramagentic behaviour should be<br /><br /><math><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math><br /><br />B. Ionic size decreases from left to right in same period.<br /><br />C. As per data, we have<br /><br /><math> <mrow>  <msup>   <mrow>    <mtext>Co</mtext></mrow>   <mrow>    <mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow>  </msup>  <mo>&#x007C;</mo><msup>   <mrow>    <mtext>Co</mtext></mrow>   <mrow>    <mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow>  </msup>  <mo>=</mo><mn>1.97</mn><mo>;</mo><mtext>&#x2009;</mtext><msup>   <mrow>    <mtext>Fe</mtext></mrow>   <mrow>    <mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow>  </msup>  <mo>&#x007C;</mo><msup>   <mrow>    <mtext>Fe</mtext></mrow>   <mrow>    <mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow>  </msup>  <mo>=</mo><mn>0.77</mn><mo>;</mo><mtext>&#x2009;</mtext><msup>   <mrow>    <mtext>Cr</mtext></mrow>   <mrow>    <mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow>  </msup>  <mo>&#x007C;</mo><msup>   <mrow>    <mtext>Cr</mtext></mrow>   <mrow>    <mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow>  </msup>  <mo>=</mo><mo>&#x2212;</mo><mn>0.41</mn></mrow></math><br /><br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is highly stable, because it does not show +2.<br /><br />D. The oxidation state increases as we go from group 3 to group 7 in same period."
